{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The development has resulted in the loss of a large family-sized dwellinghouse, the need for which is clearly demonstrated in the development plan and supporting evidence base. While it would provide a dwelling in the form of an HMO, it fails to demonstrate that this meets an identified need, and further would cause a loss of local amenity including to neighbouring occupiers. Furthermore, the proposal would have inadequate quality of accommodation which could pose harm to future residents. Additionally, the development site is not located within an area of high access to public transport and is therefore not considered an appropriate location for an HMO. As such, the proposal is considered to have a detrimental impact on the borough&#39;s housing supply and is contrary to the following policies:&nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (MHCLG, December 2024)Policies&nbsp;D6, D14 and T4&nbsp;of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP3, SP8, DMD1, DMT1, DMH5 and DMSI3&nbsp;of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan&nbsp;(September 2024)East London HMO Guidance (4)",
    "The development fails to comply with the internal space standards for single and double bedrooms. Insufficient kitchen facilities are provided. It would result in the provision of sub-standard accommodation&nbsp;and is contrary to the following policies:DCLG Technical Housing Standards (nationally described space standard) (DCLG, March 2015) (as amended)Policy D6,&nbsp; Policy H1 and H2 and Table 3.1 of the London Plan (March, 2021)Policies SP3 and DMD1 of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan (September 2024)Housing Design Standards LPG (June 2023)East London HMO Guidance (4)",
    "The HMO has the potential to increase activity level and coming and goings to and from the site leading to additional levels of noise and disturbance at the property. The proposal is therefore likely to have a negative impact on neighbouring amenity in this street of family housing and in turn the health and wellbeing of neighbouring residents. As such the proposal is contrary to the following: Policies D6, D14 and GG3 of the London Plan (March 2021) Policies DMD1, DMH5 and DMSI3 of the Local Plan (September 2024)",
    "The development fails to demonstrate&nbsp;that additional demand for parking from the development could be adequately accommodated in the surrounding area. The proposal is therefore likely to add unacceptably to parking stress and congestion in the vicinity of the application site.&nbsp; As such, it fails to comply with the following policies&nbsp;which seek to ensure proposals show how car parking will be designed and managed and to take into consideration on-street parking availability and existing and planned public transport provision:National Planning Policy Framework (December 2024)Policies T1, T6 and T6.1 of the London Plan (March 2021)"
  ],
